  "id": "Compound::DB00436 - Gene::760",
  "verbalization": "Bendroflumethiazide is a thiazide diuretic that is included in the broader class of thiazide derivatives, which are known to act on the distal convoluted tubule of the nephron to inhibit sodium reabsorption and promote potassium excretion. This mechanism is supported by multiple paths in the dataset, all of which indicate that Bendroflumethiazide binds to calcium (Ca²⁺). The commonality across these paths is the classification of Bendroflumethiazide and its derivatives as thiazide diuretics, which are linked to calcium regulation through their pharmacological action. The lowest common ancestors in all paths point to the thiazide class, reinforcing the shared mechanism of action. The evidence suggests that Bendroflumethiazide exerts its effects on calcium through a direct interaction, likely by modulating calcium homeostasis in the renal system. This relationship is further supported by the inclusion of thiazide derivatives in the dataset and their binding to calcium, indicating a consistent pharmacological profile across these compounds.",
